.Dd August 3, 2026
.Dt ROOK-PLUGIN 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rook-plugin
.Nd rook's plugin protocol
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A rook plugin is a separate process speaking newline-delimited JSON over
its own stdin and stdout.
rook spawns it, asks it questions
.Pq Cm items.list , Cm items.act ,
renders its answers in the side panel, and lets it ask two things back
.Pq Cm attention.raise , Cm session.spawn .
Every operation, in both directions, is gated by
.Em grants
declared in the configuration \(em a plugin can do exactly what its
declaration says and nothing else.
.Pp
Anything a plugin can be: a shell script, a Go binary, anything that reads
lines and writes lines.
The canonical minimal plugin,
.Pa examples/hello-plugin
in the rook repository, is 1.3 KB of POSIX sh.
.Sh DECLARING
A plugin is a node in the environment graph
.Pq see Xr rook-config 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"id": "plugin.hello", "kind": "plugin", "name": "hello",
 "command": ["/path/to/hello-plugin"],
 "load": "lazy", "grants": ["items.list", "attention.raise"]}
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width "command" -offset indent
.It Cm name
Required.
The registry key, and the cache filename for sourced plugins.
.It Cm command
Argv of a binary already on disk.
.It Cm source
A URL to fetch the binary from
.Pq Ql https:// No or Ql file:// .
Mutually exclusive with
.Cm command .
.It Cm sha256
The pin: lowercase hex digest the downloaded binary must match.
.It Cm load
.Ql eager
.Pq spawn at launch
or
.Ql lazy
.Pq spawn on first use; the default .
.It Cm grants
The ops this plugin may be asked, and may ask \(em by name, both
directions.
.El
.Pp
SDK spellings \(em Go
.Pq one struct, three strengths :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rook.Plugin{Name: "hello", Command: []string{"/path/hello"},
    Grants: []string{rook.OpItemsList}}
rook.Plugin{Source: "https://.../hello-plugin", SHA256: "459e...",
    Grants: []string{rook.OpItemsList}}
.Ed
.Pp
TypeScript
.Pq fluent :
.Ql e.plugin(name, argv, load, grants) ,
.Ql e.pluginFrom(source, grants) ,
.Ql e.pluginPinned(source, sha256, grants) .
.Sh LIFECYCLE
For a
.Cm source
plugin, first use downloads via curl to
.Pa <cache>/<name>.part
and renames into place
.Pq an interrupted fetch cannot leave a half binary that looks present ;
the cache is
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/rook/plugins/ ,
default
.Pa ~/.local/share/rook/plugins/ .
.Pp
The binary is verified on
.Em every
launch, not only after download:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
With a
.Cm sha256
pin: mismatch is refusal.
.It
Without one: trust-on-first-use \(em the first hash is recorded in a
.Pa .sha256
sidecar, and a later change is refusal
.Pq Dq changed since it was downloaded \(em delete it to accept the new one .
.It
A refusal never re-downloads.
The plugin is marked failed, its reason readable via
.Ql ctl plugins
and in the panel.
.El
.Pp
rook offers the pin rather than making you compute it: while a sourced
plugin is up and unpinned,
.Ql ctl plugins
prints its
.Ql sha256= ,
and
.Ql y
in the plugin panel copies a ready-to-paste pinned declaration
.Em in the language your config program is written in
\(em a
.Ql rook.Plugin{Source: ..., SHA256: ...}
declaration for a Go config, the
.Ql e.pluginPinned(...)
call for TypeScript.
.Pp
A spawned plugin's stderr goes to
.Pa /dev/null ;
a plugin that fails stays failed until relaunch.
The failure is a row in
.Ql ctl plugins ,
never a crash of rook.
.Sh THE PROTOCOL
Version 1.
One JSON object per line, at most 1 MiB.
Requests from rook:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"v":1,"id":7,"op":"items.list","deadlineMs":9750,
 "params":{"root":"/path/to/repo","limit":128}}
.Ed
.Pp
Replies carry the same
.Cm id :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"v":1,"id":7,"ok":true,"result":{...}}
{"v":1,"id":7,"ok":false,"error":"why"}
.Ed
.Pp
Answer within
.Cm deadlineMs
.Pq rook waits 10 s by default and gives you that minus a grace .
Frames from the plugin
.Em with
an
.Ql op
field are requests to rook; without one they are replies.
That is the whole framing rule, and it is what lets a plugin raise an
attention in the middle of answering a call.
.Ss Operations rook sends
.Bl -tag -width "items.list"
.It Cm describe
The handshake, sent once after spawn; not grant-gated.
Reply:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"v":1,"id":1,"ok":true,"result":{"name":"hello",
 "version":"0.1.0","capabilities":["items.list"]}}
.Ed
.Pp
A
.Ql v
other than 1 is refused outright.
.Cm capabilities
is what the plugin
.Em wants ;
grants are what it
.Em gets ;
.Ql ctl plugins
shows both so the gap is visible.
.It Cm items.list
Params:
.Ql root
.Pq the focused pane's repository root, possibly empty
and
.Ql limit .
Reply
.Ql result.items
.Pq plus Ql truncated bool :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"id":"pr-12","title":"fix the frobnicator","state":"running",
 "subtitle":"opened 2h ago",
 "fields":[{"key":"count","kind":"NUMBER","value":"42"}],
 "actions":[{"id":"merge","label":"Merge","confirm":true}],
 "children":[...]}
.Ed
.Pp
Everything except
.Cm id
and
.Cm title
is optional.
Caps: 128 items, 6 fields and 6 actions per item, two levels deep
.Pq children of children do not render .
The panel right-aligns field values on the row and, when width runs out,
sheds whole fields off the left of the run \(em so declare fields
.Em least important first ;
the last one survives longest.
An action with
.Cm confirm
true gets a real y/n confirm in the panel before
.Cm items.act
is sent.
An action with
.Cm input
.Ql INPUT_TEXT
opens a one-line editor under the menu \(em every printable key is
text there, ESC drops the draft, and an empty Enter refuses: a plugin
never acts on nothing.
.It Cm items.act
Params:
.Ql itemId ,
.Ql actionId ,
and
.Ql input
when the action declared it wants text.
Reply
.Ql result.message
.Pq shown in the panel
and optionally
.Ql result.item ,
the updated item; omit it and rook refetches the list instead.
.It Ar anything else
.Ql ctl plugin <name> <op> [params-json]
calls any op directly, still grant-gated \(em the escape hatch for ops the
panel has no surface for yet.
.El
.Ss Operations a plugin may send
Same frame shape, plugin's own
.Cm id ;
rook answers
.Ql {"v":1,"id":N,"ok":true}
or an error.
Each requires its grant, refused by name
.Pq Ql not granted: session.spawn .
.Bl -tag -width "attention.raise"
.It Cm attention.raise
Params:
.Ql title
.Pq required
and
.Ql body .
Lands in the attention inbox
.Pq Ql ctl attention .
Provenance is server-assigned from the declaration \(em a plugin cannot
claim to be another.
.It Cm session.spawn
Params:
.Ql command
.Pq required ,
.Ql cwd
.Pq default: the focused pane's ,
.Ql where
.Pq Ql tab No for a new tab; default a split .
Opens without stealing focus.
.It Cm session.send
Params:
.Ql pane
.Pq an id from Cm panes.activity
and
.Ql text
.Pq required, up to 8 KiB .
Types the text into that pane's pty as a bracketed paste plus Enter \(em
the ask round trip's last hop.
Two gates, both refusals by name: the pane's foreground must be an
agent TUI
.Pq claude by name or path; text typed into a shell EXECUTES ,
and a human who typed there in the last 5 seconds wins.
.It Cm clipboard.set
Params:
.Ql text
.Pq required, up to 256 KiB .
Replaces the system pasteboard \(em how a plugin hands the human text
to paste, with the human's own \(u2318V as the last hop.
The
.Cm clipboard-write
option
.Pq OSC 52's gate
is the precedent: writing the pasteboard is a permission, and this
verb's permission is its grant.
.It Cm panes.activity
No params.
The first inbound verb that answers with data:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"panes":[{"id":1,"outMs":120,"inMs":-1,"fg":"2.1.220",
  "path":"/Users/u/.local/share/claude/versions/2.1.220",
  "cwd":"/path/to/repo"}]}
.Ed
.Pp
Per terminal pane, milliseconds since the child last wrote and since the
human last typed there
.Pq physical keyboard only; \-1 = never ,
plus the foreground program \(em name and full executable path, because
the name can lie; Claude Code's versioned install runs a binary
literally named
.Ql 2.1.220
\(em and the shell's cwd.
This is the substrate's half of
.Dq is that session alive :
a transcript goes quiet during a long tool run, but the pane keeps
redrawing \(em and a human who typed there moments ago is already
watching, so a banner would be noise.
.El
.Sh THE PANEL
Open with
.Ql <leader>p
.Pq the plugin picker
or
.Ql ctl plugin-show <name> .
.Ql j / k
move,
.Ql Enter
descends into an item's actions and runs one
.Pq through its confirm if it asks for one ,
.Ql r
refetches,
.Ql y
copies the pin line,
.Ql ESC
backs out.
In a confirm, only
.Ql y
runs and only
.Ql n
or
.Ql ESC
cancels \(em a confirm that any key dismisses is not a confirm.
.Pp
The panel opens holding the keys, and ^HJKL treats it as one more
split: the away direction
.Pq ^H for a right-edge panel
hands the keys back to the panes, ^L from the edge pane walks back
in, and ^J / ^K move the selection.
.Pp
The panel's inner edge is a drag handle, like a split's separator:
pull it to resize, from a floor of 20 columns to half the window.
Rows render by kind \(em a parent is a title and clips to one line, a
child is prose and
.Em wraps ,
which is what makes a digest's bullets readable at any width.
.Pp
Children fold into the selected group: every other parent is one
headline wearing
.Ql \(u25b8+N ,
and moving the selection is what expands a group \(em headlines scan,
the group you are on reads.
The list scrolls to keep the selection on screen, and the mouse is
j/k too: a click selects its row, a click on the
.Em selected
row opens its actions.
.Sh THE SHIPPED PLUGINS
rook's first first-party plugin rides inside the bundle at
.Pa /Applications/rook.app/Contents/MacOS/rook-plugin-claude
and watches Claude Code: every recent session on the machine becomes an
item whose state is derived from the tail of the transcript Claude Code
already writes
.Pq Pa ~/.claude/projects
\(em
.Ql working ,
.Ql needs you
.Pq the turn ended, a human is next ,
.Ql blocked?
.Pq a tool call sits unanswered under a permission mode that prompts ,
.Ql idle .
When a session finishes a turn that ran longer than
.Fl -min-turn
.Pq default 30s
it raises an attention \(em the
.Dq a session is waiting on you
banner from a pane you forgot is the reason it exists.
Declare it
.Ql eager
or it cannot do that job.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rook.Plugin{Name: "claude",
    Command: []string{"/Applications/rook.app/Contents/MacOS/rook-plugin-claude"},
    Load:    rook.Eager,
    Grants:  []string{rook.OpItemsList, rook.OpItemsAct,
        rook.OpAttentionRaise, rook.OpSessionSpawn,
        "panes.activity"}}
.Ed
.Pp
Grant
.Ql panes.activity
and the transcript's word is fused with the substrate's: a pane still
redrawing its spinner keeps a quiet transcript
.Ql working
rather than
.Ql blocked?
.Pq a running tool logs nothing; the redraw is the proof of life ,
and a session whose pane you typed in moments ago never banners you \(em
you are already looking at it.
Withhold the grant and the watcher still works from transcripts alone.
.Pp
Flags, for a sandbox or a different taste:
.Fl -dir
.Pq the projects directory ,
.Fl -poll ,
.Fl -min-turn ,
.Fl -window
.Pq how far back sessions are listed; default 48h ,
.Fl -busy-rate , Fl -present , Fl -claude-names
.Pq the fusion knobs .
Measured, not guessed: a working Claude TUI writes ~500 B/s, an idle
one ~3, a dialog waiting on a human 0 \(em the default busy-rate of 200
sits between the orders of magnitude.
Actions:
.Ql open
splits a shell at the session's directory
.Pq via Cm session.spawn ,
.Ql peek
answers with the last thing the assistant said.
.Ss The agent
The second shipped plugin,
.Pa rook-plugin-agent ,
is an OpenAI-backed worker whose first job is compression: when a
Claude Code turn finishes with a reply longer than
.Fl -min-words
.Pq default 120 ,
one model call turns it into a digest \(em a headline and up to five
bullets, in the discipline of Simplified Technical English
.Pq ASD-STE100: active voice, one idea per sentence, hard word caps .
The bullets are the item's children, the bill is a
.Ql MONEY
field on the row, and the full reply stays in the session \(em the
digest makes the reading terse, never the writing.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rook.Agent{}                                  // OpenAI, gpt-5.6-luna
rook.Agent{Model: "llama3.2", API: rook.Ollama} // local, no key
rook.Agent{API: rook.APIBase("http://box:8000/v1")}
.Ed
.Pp
Each first-party plugin has a typed declaration
.Pq Vt rook.Claude , Vt rook.Agent , Vt rook.Cloud
that lowers to a plain
.Vt rook.Plugin
at emit time \(em the graph shows the flags it chose, and a
hand-written
.Vt Plugin
stays the escape hatch for any knob without a field.
.Pp
Each digest carries the reply loop:
.Ql draft a reply
spends one call writing the reply you would probably send,
.Ql expand my reply\(u2026
takes the rough words you type
.Pq Dq yeah, but keep the test
and writes the reply you meant, and
.Ql copy reply
hands the result to the pasteboard via
.Cm clipboard.set
\(em your \(u2318V into the session is the last hop, on purpose.
Drafts work from the FULL turn, never the digest.
.Pp
The API key comes from
.Ev OPENAI_API_KEY
or, because an app launched from the Dock never ran a shell profile,
from
.Fl -key-file
.Pq default Pa ~/.config/rook/openai_key .
Without one the panel says exactly that and nothing runs \(em a missing
key is configuration, not damage.
The notice belongs only to the default base: any OpenAI-compatible
server works via
.Fl -api-base
.Pq ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp all speak the same wire ,
a non-default base needs no key, and a model the price table does not
know shows no cost rather than a made-up one.
Digests live in memory only and each reply is billed at most once,
retries included; a reply that breaks the shape gets one correction and
is then taken as it came.
Flags:
.Fl -model
.Pq default Ql gpt-5-mini ,
.Fl -effort ,
.Fl -min-words , Fl -poll , Fl -window , Fl -keep ,
.Fl -max-chars
.Pq input cap per reply ,
.Fl -api-base
.Pq point it at a stub and no real money moves ,
.Fl -key-file , Fl -dir .
.Ss The cloud bridge
The third shipped plugin,
.Pa rook-plugin-cloud ,
is the thin line to rook-cloud: it holds a machine token
.Pq minted once at cloud.rookide.com, read from Ev ROOK_CLOUD_TOKEN or Fl -key-file Ns 's sibling Fl -token-file , default Pa ~/.config/rook/cloud_token ,
proves it with
.Ql GET /v1/whoami ,
and pushes a status snapshot every
.Fl -interval
.Pq default 20s :
workspaces, each with its agents and their honest states
.Pq Ql working , Ql needs_input , Ql quiet
derived from the same transcript scanner the watcher uses, fused with
.Cm panes.activity
when granted.
The fleet pages at cloud.rookide.com render it \(em your machine,
visible from your phone.
The panel row is the connection truth: connected-as, last push, or
exactly what is wrong
.Pq no token, unreachable, revoked ;
a revoked token names the fix and a re-minted token in the same file
resurrects the bridge without a restart.
.Ql push now
is the one action.
The bridge also carries the ask round trip's device half: each
needs-input agent reports an
.Ql askId ,
answers queued on the fleet page come back on the heartbeat, and a
valid answer is typed into the agent's pane via
.Cm session.send
\(em an ask that moved on
.Pq answered at the desk, or a new turn
is acked away with a note, never typed, and no answer is ever typed
twice.
.Pp
Beside the answers runs the command rail: every agent reports its
session id and its context occupancy
.Pq Ql ctxPct , measured from the transcript's own usage records ,
and the fleet page's
.Ql compact
button queues a command the bridge executes by typing
.Ql /compact
into the session's pane \(em under the same gates and the same rules,
plus one of its own: a mid-turn session is left alone and retried,
then dropped with its reason.
The cloud requests; this machine decides.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rook.Cloud{}
.Ed
.Sh A MINIMAL PLUGIN
The essence of
.Pa examples/hello-plugin ,
a sh script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/bin/sh
while IFS= read -r line; do
  id=$(expr "$line" : '.*"id":\e([0-9]*\e)')
  case $line in
  *'"op":"describe"'*)
    printf '{"v":1,"id":%s,"ok":true,"result":{"name":"hello",
      "version":"0.1.0","capabilities":["items.list"]}}\en' "$id" ;;
  *'"op":"items.list"'*)
    printf '{"v":1,"id":%s,"ok":true,"result":{"items":[
      {"id":"one","title":"hello from a plugin","state":"ok"}
      ],"truncated":false}}\en' "$id" ;;
  *)
    printf '{"v":1,"id":%s,"ok":false,
      "error":"hello only does items.list"}\en' "$id" ;;
  esac
done
.Ed
.Pp
No build step, no network, no clock \(em and it exercises the whole
machinery.
Declare it, grant
.Ql items.list ,
relaunch, open the panel.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rook 1 ,
.Xr rook-config 5 ,
.Xr rook-ctl 7
.Pp
.Pa docs/plugins/VOCABULARY.md
in the repository \(em the item model this protocol is designed against,
including the parts not yet implemented.
.Sh CAVEATS
Only the List surface renders today;
.Ql surfaces
in
.Cm describe
is accepted and ignored.
.Pp
Actions that declare an
.Cm input
are refused by name \(em rook has no form surface yet.
.Pp
The plugin set is read at launch: adding or removing a declaration takes
a relaunch.
.Pp
.Cm session.send
and
.Cm notify
appear in the vocabulary document but are not implemented.
